    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "LhuAX8eAjrgK",
        "outputId": "9f75cd1a-aa93-444d-d182-d89b3168880a"
      },
      "source": [
        "# ---- Welch's ANOVA: robust to unequal variances -----------------------\n",
        "welch_aov <- oneway.test(body_mass_g ~ species, data = peng, var.equal = FALSE)\n",
        "print(welch_aov)"
      ],
      "execution_count": 20,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "\n",
            "\tOne-way analysis of means (not assuming equal variances)\n",
            "\n",
            "data:  body_mass_g and species\n",
            "F = 316.5, num df = 2.00, denom df = 187.68, p-value < 2.2e-16\n",
            "\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Vez5hTiejrgK"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Interpretation — Section 3\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Assumptions.** Within-species normality is broadly acceptable — Chinstrap (p = 0.56) and Gentoo (p = 0.26) pass Shapiro-Wilk, Adélie is marginal (p = 0.042) — and the QQ-plots sit near the reference line. Levene's test is significant (F = 5.14, p = 0.0064) while Bartlett's is borderline (p = 0.058): Gentoo mass is more variable, so homogeneity of variance is moderately violated.\n",
        "- **ANOVA result.** F = 341.9 on df = (2, 330), p < 2e-16 → reject $H_0$. Mean body mass differs significantly across species.\n",
        "- **Effect size.** $\\eta^2 = 0.675$ — species explains about two-thirds of body-mass variance.\n",
        "- **Tukey HSD.**\n",
        "  - Gentoo − Adélie = +1386.3 g, 95% CI [1252.3, 1520.3], p < 0.001 — significant.\n",
        "  - Gentoo − Chinstrap = +1359.3 g, 95% CI [1194.4, 1524.3], p < 0.001 — significant.\n",
        "  - Chinstrap − Adélie = +26.9 g, 95% CI [−132.4, 186.2], adjusted p = 0.916 — not significant.\n",
        "- **Welch's ANOVA** (no equal-variance assumption): F = 316.5 on df = (2, 187.7), p < 2.2e-16 — same conclusion.\n",
        "- **Conclusion.** The species effect is driven almost entirely by Gentoo being much heavier. Adélie and Chinstrap are statistically indistinguishable in body mass. Reporting only \"species is significant\" would hide the most informative part of the result.\n",
        "- Because variances are unequal, Section 4 re-tests without that assumption."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "xGOLydvwjrgK"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 4. Non-Parametric Analysis — Kruskal-Wallis test"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "Vxo6iZbFjrgK",
        "outputId": "fdd38155-74de-48ba-91df-eecedfc1e0b3"
      },
      "source": [
        "# ---- Kruskal-Wallis ---------------------------------------------------\n",
        "kw <- kruskal.test(body_mass_g ~ species, data = peng)\n",
        "print(kw)\n",
        "\n",
        "cat(\"\\nMedians and IQRs by species:\\n\")\n",
        "print(data.frame(\n",
        "  species = levels(peng$species),\n",
        "  n       = as.numeric(tapply(peng$body_mass_g, peng$species, length)),\n",
        "  median  = as.numeric(tapply(peng$body_mass_g, peng$species, median)),\n",
        "  IQR     = as.numeric(tapply(peng$body_mass_g, peng$species, IQR))\n",
        "), row.names = FALSE)"
      ],
      "execution_count": 21,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "\n",
            "\tKruskal-Wallis rank sum test\n",
            "\n",
            "data:  body_mass_g by species\n",
            "Kruskal-Wallis chi-squared = 212.09, df = 2, p-value < 2.2e-16\n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
            "Medians and IQRs by species:\n",
            "   species   n median   IQR\n",
            "    Adelie 146   3700 637.5\n",
            " Chinstrap  68   3700 462.5\n",
            "    Gentoo 119   5050 800.0\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "DO64hc2UjrgK",
        "outputId": "9dbc6358-a21c-429c-c4e6-d8c0a674dbc6"
      },
      "source": [
        "# ---- Post-hoc: pairwise Wilcoxon rank-sum, Holm-adjusted --------------\n",
        "print(pairwise.wilcox.test(peng$body_mass_g, peng$species,\n",
        "                           p.adjust.method = \"holm\"))"
      ],
      "execution_count": 22,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "\n",
            "\tPairwise comparisons using Wilcoxon rank sum test with continuity correction \n",
            "\n",
            "data:  peng$body_mass_g and peng$species \n",
            "\n",
            "          Adelie Chinstrap\n",
            "Chinstrap 0.55   -        \n",
            "Gentoo    <2e-16 <2e-16   \n",
            "\n",
            "P value adjustment method: holm \n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "R8V4cfZUjrgK",
        "outputId": "bab994eb-d954-4cd0-9973-1711e655993c"
      },
      "source": [
        "# ---- Side-by-side comparison of the three approaches ------------------\n",
        "comparison <- data.frame(\n",
        "  Method    = c(\"One-way ANOVA\", \"Welch's ANOVA\", \"Kruskal-Wallis\"),\n",
        "  Statistic = c(round(F1, 2),\n",
        "                round(as.numeric(welch_aov$statistic), 2),\n",
        "                round(as.numeric(kw$statistic), 2)),\n",
        "  df        = c(paste(df1[1], df1[2], sep = \", \"),\n",
        "                paste(round(as.numeric(welch_aov$parameter), 1), collapse = \", \"),\n",
        "                as.character(kw$parameter)),\n",
        "  p_value   = c(format.pval(p1, digits = 3),\n",
        "                format.pval(welch_aov$p.value, digits = 3),\n",
        "                format.pval(kw$p.value, digits = 3)),\n",
        "  Decision  = rep(\"Reject H0\", 3),\n",
        "  stringsAsFactors = FALSE\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(comparison, row.names = FALSE)"
      ],
      "execution_count": 23,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "         Method Statistic       df p_value  Decision\n",
            "  One-way ANOVA    341.89   2, 330  <2e-16 Reject H0\n",
            "  Welch's ANOVA    316.50 2, 187.7  <2e-16 Reject H0\n",
            " Kruskal-Wallis    212.09        2  <2e-16 Reject H0\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "PTCxm32hjrgK"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Interpretation — Section 4\n",
        "\n",
        "- Kruskal-Wallis: $\\chi^2 = 212.09$, df = 2, p < 2.2e-16 — the same conclusion as ANOVA.\n",
        "- Pairwise Wilcoxon reproduces the Tukey pattern exactly: Gentoo differs from both (p < 0.001), Adélie vs Chinstrap not significant.\n",
        "- **Do the methods agree?** Yes. The mild heteroscedasticity did not change the substantive conclusion. ANOVA is fairly robust when group sizes are large and not wildly unbalanced, and Welch's ANOVA confirms this independently.\n",
        "- Note the tests answer slightly different questions — ANOVA compares means, Kruskal-Wallis compares mean ranks (distributional location). Agreement across both therefore strengthens confidence rather than being redundant."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "98a6m1d5jrgK"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 5. Two-Way ANOVA — species and sex together\n",
        "\n",
        "Model: `body_mass_g ~ species * sex`. Three nulls tested simultaneously:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. No main effect of species.\n",
        "2. No main effect of sex.\n",
        "3. No species × sex interaction."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "hp_aD9vYjrgK",
        "outputId": "72536363-24ec-4959-c5ab-492efb80f535"
      },
      "source": [
        "# ---- Cell sizes and cell means ----------------------------------------\n",
        "cat(\"Cell counts (species x sex):\\n\")\n",
        "print(table(peng$species, peng$sex))\n",
        "\n",
        "cat(\"\\nCell means (g):\\n\")\n",
        "print(round(tapply(peng$body_mass_g, list(peng$species, peng$sex), mean), 1))\n",
        "\n",
        "cat(\"\\nCell SDs (g):\\n\")\n",
        "print(round(tapply(peng$body_mass_g, list(peng$species, peng$sex), sd), 1))\n",
        "\n",
        "cat(\"\\nSex gap within each species (male - female, g):\\n\")\n",
        "cm <- tapply(peng$body_mass_g, list(peng$species, peng$sex), mean)\n",
        "print(round(cm[, \"male\"] - cm[, \"female\"], 1))"
      ],
      "execution_count": 24,
