    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "_pt5WXOgjrgM"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Interpretation — Section 5\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Species main effect:** F(2, 327) = 758.4, p < 2e-16 — consistent with Section 3.\n",
        "- **Sex main effect:** F(1, 327) = 387.5, p < 2e-16. Males are heavier within every species.\n",
        "- **Interaction (species × sex):** F(2, 327) = 8.76, p = 0.000197 — significant. This is the key finding: the *size* of the sex difference depends on species.\n",
        "- **Reading the interaction plot:** the male–female gap is 674.7 g in Adélie, 411.8 g in Chinstrap and 805.1 g in Gentoo. The lines are not parallel — the visual signature of an interaction.\n",
        "- **Biological reading.** Both species and sex shape body mass, and sexual dimorphism is most pronounced in Gentoo, the largest species — a common allometric pattern where absolute dimorphism scales with body size.\n",
        "- **Caution.** With a significant interaction, main effects should not be read in isolation; the cell means table is the honest summary."
      ]
    },
