      },
      "source": [
        "# ---- Two-way ANOVA on flipper length ----------------------------------\n",
        "aov_flip2 <- aov(flipper_length_mm ~ species * sex, data = peng)\n",
        "print(summary(aov_flip2))"
      ],
      "execution_count": 34,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "             Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value  Pr(>F)    \n",
            "species       2  50526   25263 789.912 < 2e-16 ***\n",
            "sex           1   3906    3906 122.119 < 2e-16 ***\n",
            "species:sex   2    329     165   5.144 0.00631 ** \n",
            "Residuals   327  10458      32                    \n",
            "---\n",
            "Signif. codes:  0 ‘***’ 0.001 ‘**’ 0.01 ‘*’ 0.05 ‘.’ 0.1 ‘ ’ 1\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ETX5NKCbjrgN"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Interpretation — Section 6\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Assumptions.** Shapiro-Wilk passes for Adélie (p = 0.74) and Chinstrap (p = 0.81) but not Gentoo (p = 0.0018). Unlike body mass, variances here *are* homogeneous (Levene F = 0.44, p = 0.64).\n",
        "- **ANOVA.** F = 567.4 on df = (2, 330), p < 2e-16, $\\eta^2 = 0.775$ — an even stronger species signal than body mass.\n",
        "- **Tukey HSD.** Here *all three* pairwise comparisons are significant: Chinstrap − Adélie = +5.72 mm, Gentoo − Chinstrap = +21.41 mm, Gentoo − Adélie = +27.13 mm (all p < 0.001).\n",
        "- **Kruskal-Wallis** agrees ($\\chi^2 = 237.35$, df = 2, p < 2.2e-16), as do the pairwise Wilcoxon tests (Chinstrap vs Adélie p = 6.9e-08).\n",
        "- **Consistency with body mass** — partly, and the difference is the point:\n",
        "  - Both variables identify Gentoo as clearly distinct and largest.\n",
        "  - Body mass cannot separate Adélie from Chinstrap; flipper length can.\n",
        "  - Flipper length is therefore the better single discriminator among all three species. Chinstrap penguins weigh about the same as Adélie but have measurably longer flippers — a difference in body *proportion*, not overall size.\n",
        "- **Methodological lesson.** A non-significant result on one measure does not establish equivalence. The choice of response variable determines what you are able to detect."
      ]
    },
