    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "tVPePb7XjrgN"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Overall Conclusions\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Body mass is mildly right-skewed and platykurtic overall**, but that shape is an artefact of pooling three species — the true distribution is a mixture, with Gentoo forming a separate mode.\n",
        "2. **Sex has a large, highly significant effect** (Welch t = −8.55, p = 4.8e-16, Cohen's d = −0.936, 95% CI for female − male ≈ [−840.6, −526.3] g). Males are heavier in every species.\n",
        "3. **Species has a very large effect on body mass** (F(2, 330) = 341.9, p < 2e-16, $\\eta^2 = 0.675$). Tukey HSD localises it entirely to Gentoo; Adélie and Chinstrap differ by only 26.9 g (p = 0.916).\n",
        "4. **Assumption violations mattered little.** Levene's test flagged heteroscedasticity (p = 0.0064), but Welch's ANOVA (F = 316.5, p < 2.2e-16) and Kruskal-Wallis ($\\chi^2 = 212.09$) reached identical conclusions, so the parametric analysis is trustworthy.\n",
        "5. **The two-way ANOVA reveals a significant interaction** (F(2, 327) = 8.76, p = 0.000197): sexual dimorphism is not constant across species — 674.7 g in Adélie, 411.8 g in Chinstrap, 805.1 g in Gentoo. Main effects must be read in that light.\n",
        "6. **Flipper length is a sharper species discriminator than body mass.** It separates all three species, including the Adélie/Chinstrap pair body mass cannot. Findings are consistent in direction (Gentoo largest) but flipper length carries more information.\n",
        "7. **Methodological takeaway.** Checking assumptions before choosing a test, reporting effect sizes alongside p-values, and testing more than one response variable each changed what could be concluded. The non-significant Adélie/Chinstrap mass comparison would have been easy to misread from an omnibus p-value alone."
      ]
    },
